WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.457
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.457 --> 00:00:04.290
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello,
I'm Dustin Kirkland,

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.914
product manager at
Google and your host

00:00:05.914 --> 00:00:10.290
at KubeCon Copenhagen. I'm
joined here by Khash Sajadi.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.800
He's the CEO and
founder of Cloud 66,

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.230
and he's going to tell us
a little bit about his path

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.730
to Kubernetes.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:18.630
KHASH SAJADI: Sure.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:19.980
Thank you.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.110
So we are a SAS company.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:26.170
We deliver developmental
tools for software companies.

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:28.410
And as a result of
being a SAS company,

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:31.150
we were hosting our
applications for the past four

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:34.410
and half years on AWS, CC2--

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.770
a normal setup of a cloud setup.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.500
And as part of our
products, we were

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.620
building Kubernetes
clusters for our customers

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.860
to deploy their applications.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.460
Ultimately, the rigidity of
including and introducing

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:54.870
new infrastructure components
to our own infrastructure setup

00:00:54.870 --> 00:01:00.270
that runs Cloud 66 SAS led
us to move over to running

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:03.330
Kubernetes ourselves.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.450
While we consider ourselves
experts in Kubernetes,

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:08.440
and we build that
for our customers,

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.480
we wanted to decouple
the risk and not

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.680
have too much of an
inception, if you will.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.150
So we moved to the
best Kubernetes

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.460
that we could find
outside of Cloud 66,

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.110
and that was Google GKE.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:24.150
So the migration planning for
us took about three to four

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:28.830
months, and most of it was spent
around risk mitigation and data

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:31.560
transfer planning for
our customers to have--

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.729
we have customers in
about 120 countries.

00:01:33.729 --> 00:01:34.770
So there's no night time.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:36.030
There's no downtime.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:40.290
We had to do that in a seamless
way as much as possible.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.097
And then the migration
itself took a day.

00:01:42.097 --> 00:01:43.179
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Wow.

00:01:43.179 --> 00:01:44.970
KHASH SAJADI: So we
planned the whole thing

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:46.650
and we did the entire
thing in a day.

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.410
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So three
to four months planning,

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:50.700
a day to do the migration?

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.320
KHASH SAJADI: Yes.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.740
So our move to GKE was smooth.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.650
As a result, it cut our
infrastructure costs

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:01.150
by about 60%.

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:04.770
But I think the best
takeaway that we took away

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.440
from moving to Kubernetes,
and GKE in this instance,

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:11.430
was it added to the agility of
our production and operations

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:12.875
team.

00:02:12.875 --> 00:02:14.250
We wanted to be
able to introduce

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:15.930
new components and
joint infrastructure,

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.640
like search components.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.250
And we didn't want
to learn maintenance

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.940
of yet another product, or
having to go and outsource

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:27.510
that from third-party
vendors all around,

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.580
making us more
dependent on a variety

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.142
of different components.

00:02:31.142 --> 00:02:33.150
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Now,
that 60% cost savings

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:35.880
on the infrastructure,
is that primarily savings

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.359
on the management layer?

00:02:38.359 --> 00:02:40.650
KHASH SAJADI: There is a part
that's not very tangible,

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.300
and that's how much
time we spend less

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.360
on managing infrastructure,
which is on another P&amp;L.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.060
Which I wouldn't
know much about.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:48.810
I don't have that much
visibility over it,

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:52.080
because it's kind of
funny money in a way.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.590
But when it comes
to spend, we now

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:58.110
tend to spin up bigger
boxes, as in VMs,

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.360
and pack them much
more efficiently.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.610
Before we had one component
that would take over

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:05.790
the entire server but
would be utilized at 20%.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:10.980
Now we can move workloads around
and make it more flexible.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:11.940
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:12.440
OK.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:14.640
So Kubernetes has
helped Cloud 66.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.950
Tell me a little bit
about Cloud 66's business.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:19.987
What do you guys
do with Kubernetes?

00:03:19.987 --> 00:03:22.320
KHASH SAJADI: So our SAS
product-- we have two products.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.840
One is scaffolding and
building Kubernetes clusters

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.660
for our customers, but our
focus is not doing that.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:30.740
As building the container
side of the story,

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.190
we are mostly
focused on workloads

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:34.606
that you don't run inside of
the containers, like database

00:03:34.606 --> 00:03:37.320
backups, replication, firewall,
audit management, access

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:38.090
to service.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:40.110
That's called Cloud 66 Maestro.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:43.170
And we actually built a
product out of our experience

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:46.845
of moving to Kubernetes and
managing our deployments

00:03:46.845 --> 00:03:50.160
through Kubernetes, which
we call Cloud 66 Skycap

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.690
or container delivery pipeline.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:55.290
We had to make sure two
things happen before we

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:57.100
can move to Kubernetes.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:58.960
One is the technical
engineering side of it,

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:00.251
which you have one application.

00:04:00.251 --> 00:04:02.168
You usually break it
apart into microservices,

00:04:02.168 --> 00:04:03.792
and then you wrap
them into containers,

00:04:03.792 --> 00:04:05.460
and you write a bunch
of configuration

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:06.780
files for Kubernetes to run.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:07.920
That part is the easy part.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.224
As an engineering team, we are
fairly capable of doing that.

00:04:11.224 --> 00:04:13.140
The part that we had to
make sure that happens

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:16.200
is, do we have the
toolset to change

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:19.380
the mentality of the team that
not the entire application goes

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:20.467
out at the same time?

00:04:20.467 --> 00:04:22.050
Somethings are
dependent on other ones

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:23.550
when the deployment happens.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.480
How do we know that
now that we have

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:27.780
two version controls,
essentially, one Git and one

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.900
Docker registry, for example,
we can trace and track things

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.280
down?

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.070
We can track and
trace things down

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:36.540
in terms of the
assets that are now

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:39.750
broken apart into hundreds of
containers of micro services.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.960
How can we take care
of security secrets

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.520
and also make sure that our
IP is not linked into one

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:49.585
of the layers of Docker image?

00:04:49.585 --> 00:04:51.960
All of that leading in to
actually building a product out

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:52.752
of that experience.

00:04:52.752 --> 00:04:55.001
We are a product company,
and we provide that as a SAS

00:04:55.001 --> 00:04:56.220
or offering to our customers.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:00.735
So we took that opportunity
and productized our learnings.

00:05:00.735 --> 00:05:02.610
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Now,
application development

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:05.860
on Kubernetes takes a bit
of a different approach.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:08.580
What did you learn on that
aspect of your journey,

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:11.760
on your road to Kubernetes?

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:14.550
KHASH SAJADI: That's
a very good question.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:18.240
So in previous life, I've
worked as a software architect

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.410
for larger enterprises,
mostly investment banks,

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:24.120
and what I've experienced
was very, very, similar

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:27.330
to the experience that I
had with Martin Fowler's way

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:30.520
of doing microservices and
service-oriented architecture,

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:32.229
if you will, based on contracts.

00:05:32.229 --> 00:05:34.020
It doesn't necessarily
have to be broken up

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:35.560
into multiple services.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.520
But we found that applying
those principles when

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:41.220
it comes to
contract-based development

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:45.570
and having a definitive
internal API between services

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:47.910
always helps, regardless
of whether that codebase is

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:49.995
one codebase or
you break it up out

00:05:49.995 --> 00:05:51.962
into physical
different codebases.

00:05:51.962 --> 00:05:52.920
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Great.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.512
So I understand that we've
got a bit of a demo here?

00:05:55.512 --> 00:05:56.220
KHASH SAJADI: Yes

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:57.460
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: All
right, let's see it.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:58.320
KHASH SAJADI: Great.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:03.480
OK, so we have, as part
of Cloud 66 Skycap,

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:06.570
which is container delivery
pipeline into Kubernetes,

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:09.360
an application that is
just one simple service,

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:10.890
and I wanted to
deploy that to GKE.

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:13.020
So here's what we do.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:15.670
We have a concept
of a formation.

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:19.720
And formation for us is
a deployment destination.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:21.750
It's like, environments
don't have it anymore,

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:24.170
because you might have five
productions and 12 stagings

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:26.320
just because you can do
that based on a Kubernetes

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:27.890
namespace.

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:29.460
We do that.

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:35.210
And here, I'm adding different
parts of the application

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:36.350
based on templates--

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:39.470
Kubernetes native templates--
that we have in a Git

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:40.310
repository.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:44.450
The idea for us was to not
wrap the Kubernetes API

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:45.520
into yet another API.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:48.470
To just control the access
of our team to Kubernetes,

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:50.510
we wanted developers
to have the full access

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:53.030
and experience the full
benefits of Kubernetes

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:54.800
when it comes to templates.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:57.530
But also, for the ops
teams and ops people

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:00.080
to have a say over how
things are deployed.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.960
So here I'm setting up a
namespace from my Kubernetes

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:06.680
information.

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:08.980
And here, I'm doing a
deployment of a service.

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:12.410
So this is a normal
Kubernetes deployment asset,

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:14.120
as you can see.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:18.020
And the idea is that my
ops team will dictate,

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:22.250
if you want to deploy or add
a deployment to Kubernetes,

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:23.487
how should it look like.

00:07:23.487 --> 00:07:25.070
For example, if
you're running on GKE,

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:27.650
you might want to run
on Cloud SQL as well,

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:29.435
and therefore you
need cloud proxy

00:07:29.435 --> 00:07:31.310
as a side car for
production, but this is not

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:32.520
a concern for staging.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.740
Therefore, my ops
guys can dictate that,

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:36.890
I'm going to add the
port for my service,

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:41.240
enter to the command that
starts it in a normal Kubernetes

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:43.050
or Docker-friendly way.

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:46.760
In this instance, I'm
running RADIUS directly here

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:48.770
as a container.

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:50.970
And this is all backed
by a Git repository,

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:57.440
so you can use normal Git flow
as opposed to using the web UI.

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:02.010
And the last step for this
is to create a service that

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:03.560
represents that deployment.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:06.170
Again, this is more ops
teams' opinion on how

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:07.740
the service should look like.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:09.890
And this comes from a
Git repository itself,

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:12.440
so you can evolve this.

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:14.990
Here, because I'm deploying to
GKE, instead of a node balance

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:17.580
I'm using a load balance there.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:24.350
So GKE will fire that up
for me and save the changes.

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:25.930
And because we were
running RADIUS,

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:29.415
we are going to run RADIUS
inside of my cluster as well.

00:08:29.415 --> 00:08:30.790
I'm going to just
fire up RADIUS.

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:33.070
Now, this could be a Helm
chart that I can get off

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:36.070
the shelf and just
deploy RADIUS,

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:38.650
or it could be my team's
opinion on how RADIUS should

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:41.419
be deployed in my organization.

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:44.200
And once I have all those
components, what happens

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:47.950
is what we call taking a
snapshot of your application,

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:49.480
which will take
a snapshot of all

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:51.430
your codebase, all
pre-built images

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:53.210
that you have for
your application,

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:55.360
and then all of
those will result

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:58.690
in a rendered set of Kubernetes
native configuration files

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:01.420
that you can then
apply to your cluster.

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:03.700
I'm just going to
get the command that

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:04.600
will generate that.

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:06.430
We do support
multi-accounts, so I'm just

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:10.310
going to switch it
to our demo account,

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:14.086
and then paint that
onto a Kube control

00:09:14.086 --> 00:09:15.460
and apply it to
my cluster, which

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:17.070
is the GKE Cluster that I have.

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:25.970
So the namespace--
the secret, which

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:28.850
would be the Docker registry
that we provide, all of that

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:31.640
is going to get created.

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:34.040
The namespace is there now.

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:35.240
And let's check the pods.

00:09:39.791 --> 00:09:40.290
Great.

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:41.170
So the pods are running.

00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:42.510
And I'm going to
just quickly check

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:44.510
the service to make sure
to look balancer is up.

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:48.330
OK.

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:51.570
So the IP address is
now getting started.

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:55.080
I'm going to just
put a watch on it.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:55.680
It's up now.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:56.180
OK.

00:09:56.180 --> 00:10:00.690
So I'm just going to get the
IP address that GKE gave me,

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:02.670
and there we are.

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:04.630
So my app is up and running.

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:07.050
But most importantly-- this
was a simple app, obviously.

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:10.350
But this is based on the
opinion that my operations guys

00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:11.340
want me to have.

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:12.900
And I can then
switch this formation

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:15.570
into another
deployment target that

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:17.840
is running a homemade
Kubernetes cluster that

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:19.500
doesn't require certain things.

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:22.710
Or my ops guys can
force my developers

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:25.494
to every time they add a
new deployment, for example,

00:10:25.494 --> 00:10:27.910
they need to have a sidecar
for log shipping, for example.

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:30.780
So it gives you the
flexibility of a Kubernetes API

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:35.760
without the risks of being
able to change things

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:37.844
in the entire cluster that
you're not supposed to.

00:10:37.844 --> 00:10:38.760
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:40.020
That's super interesting.

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:42.930
I can see how this would
drastically improve and speed

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:44.704
up some development cycles.

00:10:44.704 --> 00:10:46.870
All right, Khash, thank you
very much for your time.

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:47.470
Cheers

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:50.820
[MUSIC PLAYING]

